<h2>The case in one view</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Case</th><td>Pushkar Pandey v. Govt of NCT of Delhi, BAIL APPLN. 4249/2026</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Court</th><td>High Court of Delhi, Mini Pushkarna J</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Decided</th><td>5 November 2026</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Provisions</th><td>Sections 21, 37 and 67, NDPS Act; anticipatory bail under Section 482 BNSS</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Outcome</th><td>Anticipatory bail granted on conditions</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The Anti-Narcotics Squad arrested one co-accused with roughly 4 grams of cocaine and 20 grams of smack. In his disclosure statement, the co-accused named others and alleged that sale proceeds were routed into the petitioner&rsquo;s bank account on the instructions of a third person. The prosecution&rsquo;s file against the petitioner was built on that disclosure, the financial transactions, and a large volume of calls between the petitioner and the third person.</p>
<p>The Special Court refused anticipatory bail in April 2026. The co-accused himself obtained regular bail in July 2026. Non-bailable warrants were issued against the petitioner in September 2026, and the State characterised him as an absconder. The petitioner — twenty-four years old, with a clean record and not named in the FIR — maintained that the monetary dealings were loans and undertook to cooperate with the investigation.</p>

<h2>The disclosure-statement problem</h2>
<p>The legal spine of the decision is the evidentiary status of a co-accused&rsquo;s disclosure. Following the Supreme Court in <em>Balwinder Singh (Binda) v. NCB</em> and coordinate-bench authority of the Delhi High Court, the court held that a disclosure statement, absent substantive corroboration, does not establish a direct link between the petitioner and the contraband. The remaining material was for the trial to evaluate:</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> the financial and call records material &ldquo;is a matter of trial and cannot be a ground to deny anticipatory bail.&rdquo;</p>
</div>

<h2>Why Section 37 did not bite</h2>
<p>Section 37 NDPS imposes twin conditions on bail where commercial quantities are involved. Here, the seizure was from the co-accused, and no direct involvement of the petitioner with the seized quantity was shown; the court accordingly held that Section 37&rsquo;s rigours were not attracted to his plea. Concerns about witnesses and cooperation were met through conditions — joining the investigation, passport surrender, an operational mobile number, and no contact with witnesses — on a personal bond of Rs 25,000 with one surety.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For accused persons named in disclosures.</strong> The distinction between being named and being linked is the whole case. Where there is no recovery and no independent corroboration, anticipatory bail remains realistically available even in NDPS matters.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>On NBWs and absconder labels.</strong> Process issued after a rejected bail plea does not automatically foreclose the High Court&rsquo;s jurisdiction. A credible undertaking to join the investigation, backed by a clean record, can still carry the day — though the safer course is always to appear and cooperate early.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For the prosecution.</strong> The decision is a reminder that building a syndicate case on disclosures and bank entries alone invites bail at the threshold. Corroborative recovery or independent evidence of dealing is what keeps Section 37 in play.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>The NDPS Act&rsquo;s severity makes the quality-of-evidence question at the bail stage unusually decisive. The Supreme Court&rsquo;s jurisprudence — from the confessional-statement line in <em>Tofan Singh</em> to <em>Balwinder Singh</em> — has steadily insisted that liberty cannot be made to turn on a co-accused&rsquo;s say-so. This decision applies that insistence at the anticipatory stage, where the stakes are the arrest itself. For practitioners in Delhi, it is a current, citable instance of the High Court distinguishing between the investigative utility of a disclosure and its legal sufficiency to justify custody.</p>
